he minor allele  and so genetics data  sometimes could come in the form of two  lines per person with these  letters a t c  or g  but more often the way it comes is  in one line and at a particular spot or  locus  there will be a number of how many  copies a person has of the minor allele  so  um  if  there are  tnt  on both lines there will be two copies  of and and t is the minor allele will be  two copies and in this case it'll be a  one one copy or  in the case of where a and a will be  zero copies and then there'll be another  file called a map file  that has all these  spots or locus  enumerated and then  the second column that tells which is  the minor allele at optical response  um  so  so i was given a program  that would generate this type of  genetics  data  and a trade and it would take about like  20 parameters  and then it would produce  four different files one at the family  level and  three at the siblings level  and the  the the basic  idea was that some of these parameters  didn't vary from simulation to  simulation like  the total number of families how many  children each family would have the  number of snips or  loci and  there are these other parameters that  i was supposed to give to the model it  was a very complicated model  the program was written by a biologist  who  took into account  all kinds of theories  that i don't know anything about  but i was given a list of parameters um  then i just decided that  i should make 10 simulations  and of course with all these  random numbers being generated i need to  keep track of random seats so i could  reproduce things so we had total of 50  000  of families and 1200 snips that's like  the gist of this slide  but then there's this other  parameters that um they're like broken  into sets of two  i'm not gonna  go into too much detail about them but  they  had  values that had to vary  and  one of them had  had to do with heritability which  people define as the  proportion of variation  of the trade that is explained by genes  so there were two of those parameters  and they had to  you would try low medium and high values  for them  and then there's like  two parameters for correlation  between siblings  of  some part of the environment and then  there were two  parameters for these um  like dominance part of the model  and  early on um it was decided that  one of them would just keep at zero and  not vary  but  you can calculate quickly there's like  72 combinations so it's not a huge  number  but  something that  i had to  work with  okay the analysis that i had to run  had a very similar flow to the  simulations so  i get the simulated files prep them  make like an input file  to the analysis program and then i would  get some results  and so the goal is to simulate 10 cents  for each parameter combination and um to  begin with i said okay let me take one  of them and just run it  on a linux server  and it took eight hours  to simulate the data and for just about  10 gigabytes of data which is not a huge  amount but you know it adds up  and i'm like okay well let's then um run  the analysis program uh some  one of which i wrote and that  one took about two hours to run  so i thought okay  i need to do something  here  but before i tell you what i decided to  do which you already know what it was  let me kind of tell you what kind of  analysis people run on these types of  data  so the  the very first um  and it's still being run type analysis  that people run is to discover snips  that means to discover snips that are  associated with the given trait and to  do that they run thing as something  called genome-wide association studies  which basically is like a  regression or association models that  would  use all  available  genetics data and see  if any  snip or locus  has a  significant association with the outcome  and um usually the results are come  plotted in something called the  manhattan plot  and people use a very very stringent  p-value  beca