e a classic case for uh trying  to do things in parallel  and um  i realized that a lot of people probably  at this conference use big data and are  very used to parallel processing so i  apologize this is  nothing new um  is my one sentence about parallel  processing um which is basically  executing repeated tests simultaneously  on different cores or on different nodes  on a cluster  so as i mentioned i started by running  things on our  linux server which um  soon after i realized had some  limitations  so  then i had to move  the simulations to  high power computing settings which  this is my very  basic picture of it  which stacks  um a lot of cores  or processing power  in one place  but um  we're on a lot of computers nowadays  they come with multiple cores  i like this picture because uh it shows  you the course that they're kind of  separate from each other and they don't  really talk to each other  with the high power computing people  program  ways that they could talk to each other  so  you could even run  not quite independent tasks  on them  um so how i did this in r was um  i used the for each package in the two  parallel package um and the forage  package  um if you're not familiar with it  it runs pretty much like a for loop in  our if you use it with the do operator  except  your  output would probably get produced it  will get back to you as a list  but um  the 4-h loop has the capability to use  the dual power operator which  allows each like iteration of the loop  to  use a different core  but you have to tell it how many  clusters or how many cores or workers  you want to use and then you have to  register them so  from then on my job is very easy i would  just put all my parameters  into separate vectors and then  call them right before i call my main  simulation program  and  then  if you use a do parallel package it's a  good idea to stop your cluster sound  so that your memory gets cleared  so  that's what i wanted to share with you  today 